{"graph_chars": "Labor markets have demand and supply curves. The law of demand: a higher wage cuts the quantity of labor employers want. The law of supply: a higher wage raises the quantity workers offer. Minneapolis-Saint Paul-Bloomington, two thousand twenty: about forty-one thousand registered nurses. Table four point one. Equilibrium salary eighty-five thousand dollars a year. Equilibrium quantity forty-one thousand nurses. Quantity can be workers or hours. Price is compensation: salary plus benefits, and benefits can be as high as thirty percent. At ninety thousand, forty-five thousand people want the job, employers want forty thousand: a surplus of labor. At seventy thousand, employers want fifty-two thousand, only twenty-seven thousand will work: a shortage. Higher pay draws nurses from other cities, into training, and into full-time work. Facing higher salaries, hospitals may substitute computer monitors and lower-paid aides.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0652], [0.0652, 0.1304], [0.1304, 0.1956], [0.1956, 0.2608], [0.2608, 0.326], [0.326, 0.3912], [0.3912, 0.4564], [0.4564, 0.5216], [0.5216, 0.5868], [0.5868, 0.652], [0.652, 0.7172], [0.7172, 0.7824], [0.7824, 0.8476], [0.8476, 0.9128], [0.9128, 0.978], [0.978, 1.0432], [1.0432, 1.1084], [1.1084, 1.1736], [1.1736, 1.2388], [1.2388, 1.304], [1.304, 1.3692], [1.3692, 1.4344], [1.4344, 1.4996], [1.4996, 1.5648], [1.5648, 1.63], [1.63, 1.6952], [1.6952, 1.7604], [1.7604, 1.8256], [1.8256, 1.8908], [1.8908, 1.956], [1.956, 2.0212], [2.0212, 2.0864], [2.0864, 2.1516], [2.1516, 2.2168], [2.2168, 2.282], [2.282, 2.3472], [2.3472, 2.4124], [2.4124, 2.4776], [2.4776, 2.5427], [2.5427, 2.6079], [2.6079, 2.6731], [2.6731, 2.7383], [2.7383, 2.8035], [2.8035, 2.8687], [2.8687, 2.9339], [2.9339, 2.9991], [2.9991, 3.0643], [3.0643, 3.1295], [3.1295, 3.1947], [3.1947, 3.2599], [3.2599, 3.3251], [3.3251, 3.3903], [3.3903, 3.4555], [3.4555, 3.5207], [3.5207, 3.5859], [3.5859, 3.6511], [3.6511, 3.7163], [3.7163, 3.7815], [3.7815,